 Technicians enter an equipment serial number and need its calibration date, due date, and equipment description. A signed snapshot contains newline-delimited JSON records, a schema version, a snapshot date, and a signature manifest.
 
+The registry answers every lookup from files on the kiosk. Each kiosk turns an arriving snapshot into its own lookup index, so a kiosk's answers depend only on the last snapshot it imported successfully.
+
 ## Architecture
 
-Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
+Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed. The certificates node uses only this interface.
+
+Snapshot validation, index building, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is an embedded SQLite index: one database file per imported snapshot, stored on the kiosk's local disk and opened in-process. Index files, their schema, and their paths are private to this node.
+
+## Index
+
+Each index file holds the records of exactly one snapshot, keyed by normalized serial number (see Lookup Behavior), together with that snapshot's date and schema version. An index file is never modified after it is built; a new snapshot produces a new file rather than updating the existing one.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Exactly one index file is active at a time. A small pointer on local disk names the active file, and it is the only thing activation changes.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Import runs in this order, and stops at the first failure:
+
+1. Verify the signature manifest against the bundled public key.
+2. Reject a schema version the kiosk does not support.
+3. Build a new index file in a staging location on the kiosk's local disk, reading the snapshot's records and rejecting duplicate serial numbers.
+4. Activate the new index by replacing the active pointer in a single atomic file-system step.
+
+Nothing is read from the snapshot's records until the signature has been verified. The staged index is invisible to lookup until step 4, so a lookup sees either all of the previous snapshot or all of the new one. A failure at any step discards the staged file, leaves the previous index active, and reports which check failed.
+
+Lookups continue against the previous index while an import is running.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Look the normalized serial up by key in the active index, opened read-only, and return the index's snapshot date whether or not a record matches.
+
+A keyed read of one row from a 90,000-row index is well inside the 250 ms goal on a 4 GB kiosk, and its cost does not depend on which serial is requested.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Embedded SQLite index built on each kiosk | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | Lookup must complete from files on the kiosk with no runtime network access, and kiosks have no network route. Both alternatives answer lookups over a network. The cost accepted is that every kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index for each snapshot. |
+| Applying a new snapshot | Build a separate index file, then switch the active pointer | Update the active index in place inside a transaction | A failed or interrupted import can only ever leave an unused staged file behind, so the last valid snapshot stays available without relying on rollback. Costs temporary disk space for a second index. |
+
